      "content": "Apologies for the confusion earlier. Let me clarify those aspects of morphology for your words with more precision.\n\n1. Talossani: This word does indeed have a complex structure. \"Talo\" is the root meaning \"house.\" The prefix \"to-\" often denotes possession in some agglutinative languages and could signify an attributive or possessive quality. \"San\" denotes the first person singular present tense, while the suffix \"-ni\" indicates the allative case towards the speaker, effectively translating to \"to my house\" if we consider it in a possessive sense. In this construction, we indeed have vowel harmony and phonotactic constraints at play. The vowel 'a' in \"talo\" harmonizes with the preceding 'o' of the prefix to follow the language's vowel harmony rules, which typically would have 'o' as a front vowel, although 'a' can appear as a front vowel in some cases due to the influence of 's' which can affect vowel articulation. The stem changes could come into play due to agglutinative morphology where inflectional suffixes are added to the root through a process that can change vowel or consonant sounds at morpheme boundaries for ease of pronunciation.\n\n2",
